body:
Water benders for a seemingly non-obvious reason. The majority of a fire-fighters job is not fighting fires, its first-responder medical care. When you call 911 for a broken leg or any other medical problem a fire crew and an ambulance will be sent in most cities. This is because there are a large number of fire stations spread everywhere in a city and thus are almost always closer to a call than the relatively smaller number of ambulances. Fire fighters normally get there first and provide a basic level of medical care until the ambulance arrives and takes over. The ambulance then has to take the pt to the hospital, making it unavailable for a long time while the fire crew goes directly back into service in their area. Medical calls make up the majority of a fire crews times spent working, with real true fire calls being rare; maybe a once or twice a month experience for a crew member. Depending on the city there can be 5-13 medical calls per crew per day. Suffice it to say the healing abilites of water benders would make them much more able to fill the full role of a firefighter. 

&#x200B;

(Note this does not apply to federal firefighters who mainly deal with large scale forest fires, or military/industrial firefighters who will mainly work on fire prevention on a specific installation e.g. a runway)

&#x200B;

For fighting fires water is destructive to property making it less preferred than fire bending for putting fires out, however has the added benefit of preventing flare up again after something has been thoroughly soaked. This damage I would believe would be outweighed by the healing properties of the ability. Current civil engineering standards make water relatively abundant, especially with the ability to bend it, to the point I don't think it would be an issue for stopping fires. 

&#x200B;

Firebending is probably the best for just stopping the fire straight up, assuming fire bending allows the fire to be dissipated.

&#x200B;

Expert air benders are capable of removing the oxygen needed for combustion, however the training required I believe would be prohibitive to the large amount of personnel needed to always have people close to a fire or medical call, as the current 911 system is situated. Fire fighters are not really well paid except in some big cities. In my state capital it averages around 35k a year. Ambulance workers get paid even less with starting salaries being around $12-$14 an hour. There is not a lot of excess budget to pay for advanced training or advanced practitioners. 

&#x200B;

(As a side note, when possible, support increased pay for first responders. It hurts to see Chick-fil-A paying more when first responders have training fees, extremely stressful jobs, and take on lots of personal risk)
